  "title": "Luo migrations into the Great Lakes region",
  "summary": "Luo-speaking peoples began to expand from the Bahr el-Ghazal region of South Sudan in the mid-second millennium CE. One group under Labong’o entered Bunyoro and founded its ruling Bito dynasty; from Pawir the Luo spread across Acholi and Lango and southeast to Busoga. Another group moved east beyond Mount Elgon, ancestral to the Padhola of Uganda and the Dholuo of western Kenya, who settled by the eastern shore of Lake Victoria.",
